course: history
lesson: 11
title: Ancient Greece: city-states, Athens and Sparta
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Greece is full of mountains, sea and islands, so instead of one country the Greeks lived in hundreds of separate city-states, each called a polis, with its own government, army and laws.
explain: They often quarrelled, like neighbouring schools with their own rules and uniforms, but they shared a language, the same gods and the Olympic Games, and joined together against outside enemies.
explain: The two most famous were Athens, a city of traders, sailors, thinkers and artists, and Sparta, a city of soldiers where boys began training at seven.
explain: Together they faced the mighty Persian Empire at Marathon and Thermopylae. Some stories about these times, like the wooden horse of Troy, the Minotaur or the film 300, are legend or fantasy, and a good historian says which is which.
fact: Greece is full of mountains and islands, so the Greeks lived in many separate city-states rather than one country.
fact: Each city-state, or polis, had its own government, army, laws and favourite god, but all Greeks shared a language, gods and the Olympic Games.
fact: On Crete the Minoans built the palace of Knossos with many rooms about 3,700 years ago, and they loved the sea and bull-leaping.
fact: The Minotaur is a myth, but bull-leaping was a real sport or ritual of the Minoans.
fact: Homer's poems tell of the Greeks besieging Troy for ten years and of the hero Odysseus's long journey home; the wooden horse is a story from legend, not proven history.
fact: Athens was a city of traders, sailors, thinkers and artists, with the Parthenon temple to the goddess Athena on its rocky hill, the Acropolis.
fact: In Sparta boys left home at seven to train hard as soldiers, and Spartan girls also did sports to be strong.
fact: Sparta relied on a huge enslaved people, the helots, to farm for them.
fact: Athens loved debate, trade, art and its navy; Sparta loved discipline, obedience and its army.
fact: In 490 BC the Athenians beat a Persian army at Marathon; the story of a runner racing to Athens with the news is a legend told much later.
fact: In 480 BC King Leonidas with 300 Spartans and several thousand other Greeks held the narrow pass of Thermopylae against a huge Persian army for three days; in so narrow a place only a few enemies could fight at once.
fact: Greeks sailed in triremes, fast warships with three rows of oars, and traded olive oil, wine and pottery.
exercise: Why did the Greeks live in many city-states instead of one country? | Mountains and sea split the land into separate parts | A law said no country could be big | They all spoke different languages | They had no idea what a country was
exercise: What was a polis? | A Greek city-state with its own government | A Greek temple standing high on a hill | A Greek warship with oars | A Greek silver coin
exercise: What did all the Greek city-states share? | A language, gods and the Olympic Games | One king and one army for all of Greece | The same laws written down everywhere | The same capital city
exercise: On which island did the Minoans live? | Crete | Sicily | Britain | Cyprus
exercise: What was the palace of Knossos famous for? | Its many rooms | Its golden roof | Being made of ice | Its giant wall around Greece
exercise: Which part of the Minoan story is real? | Bull-leaping as a sport or ritual | The Minotaur, half man and half bull | The flying boy Icarus | A monster under the palace
exercise: Who wrote the poems about the Trojan War? | Homer | Plato | Julius Caesar | Confucius
exercise: How long did the Greeks besiege Troy in the poems? | Ten years | Ten days | One hundred years | One night
exercise: What is the Parthenon? | A temple to Athena on the Acropolis | A palace for the Spartan king and his guards | A theatre in Rome | A ship with three rows of oars
exercise: At what age did Spartan boys leave home to train? | Seven | Fifteen | Twenty | Two
exercise: Who farmed the land for the Spartans? | The helots, an enslaved people | Persian farmers paid by the Spartan kings | Robots built by clever Greek inventors | Athenian sailors sent from the port
exercise: What did Athens love most? | Debate, trade, art and its navy | Only fighting on land with spears | Silence, obedience and soldiers' drill | Keeping all foreigners out of the city
exercise: Who won the battle of Marathon in 490 BC? | The Athenians | The Persians | The Romans | The Spartans alone
exercise: Who held the pass of Thermopylae in 480 BC? | Leonidas with 300 Spartans and thousands of other Greeks | Only 300 Spartans, with nobody else at all to help | Alexander the Great and his army | The Persian king alone
exercise: What was a trireme? | A fast warship with three rows of oars | A temple with three tall columns in a row | A three-wheeled chariot | A three-day festival for Zeus
exercise: What did the Greeks trade? | Olive oil, wine and pottery | Potatoes, tomatoes and chocolate | Silk and paper from China | Oil from wells and coal
test: bro watched 300 and says only 300 guys fought at Thermopylae. right? | No, several thousand other Greeks fought too | Yes, exactly 300 and no more, the film is right | Yes, and they all lived | No, there were 3 million Spartans
test: Your mate says the wooden horse of Troy is proven history. What is fair to say? | It is a story from legend, not proven history | It is in every history book as a proven fact | Archaeologists dug up the whole wooden horse | Homer filmed it
test: If Greece were one flat plain, what might have been different? | It might have been easier to unite into one country | There would be no sea anywhere near it | The Greeks would all have spoken Chinese | Nobody would ever have wanted to live there
test: Two city-states argue a lot but a Persian army comes. What did the Greeks often do? | Join together against the outside enemy | Join the Persians at once and fight their neighbours | Leave Greece for ever | Ask Rome for help
test: Why did Sparta need the helots? | The Spartans trained as soldiers and did not farm | The helots were the kings and rulers of Sparta | The helots were their teachers | Sparta had no land at all to farm
test: Why would Athens want a strong navy? | It lived by trade and the sea | It had no coast at all | Ships were cheaper than shoes | The gods banned armies
test: The Marathon runner story. What do historians say? | The battle is real, the runner story is a later legend | Both the battle and the runner are made up | The runner was really the Persian king | The race only happened in 2004 at the Olympics
test: Minotaur in my game, half bull half man. Real or nah? | A myth; the bull-leaping was real | A myth; nobody ever leapt over bulls on Crete | Real, found in Crete in 1900 | Real, but it was a cow
test: Spartan girls did sports too. Why? | Spartans wanted them to be strong | Girls were the only soldiers in Sparta | They were training to row in the navy | It was a punishment for being lazy
test: Which city would a sailor, an artist and a thinker most like to live in? | Athens | Sparta | Persepolis | Troy
test: Why could a narrow pass help a small army against a huge one? | Only a few enemies can fight at once there | Narrow passes are always uphill both ways for enemies | Big armies cannot see in mountains | The pass was full of food
exam: Identify the principal reason for which ancient Greece consisted of numerous independent city-states rather than a unified realm. | The terrain was divided by mountains and sea | A royal decree forbade unification | The inhabitants spoke mutually unintelligible languages | The Persians prohibited it
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the battle of Thermopylae in 480 BC. | The Spartans were accompanied by several thousand other Greeks | Exactly three hundred combatants took part on the Greek side | The Persians were defeated within one hour | The battle was fought at sea near Troy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contrast between Athens and Sparta? | Athens esteemed debate, commerce and its navy; Sparta esteemed discipline and its army | Both cities esteemed solely their navies and despised armies | Sparta was renowned for its philosophers and theatre | Athens forbade all commerce
exam: Ascertain the status of the helots within Spartan society. | An enslaved population compelled to cultivate the land | The hereditary kings of Sparta | Foreign merchants resident in Athens | Priests of Athena upon the Acropolis
